<td id="S3.T3.5.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.00%</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.5.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Causal LM + Recurrence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="S3.T3.5.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">11.30%</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.02%</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.5.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T3.5.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Base</span> (this work)</th>
<td id="S3.T3.5.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.18%</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.08%</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.5.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S3.T3.5.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Bijective</span> (this work)</th>
<td id="S3.T3.5.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2.32%</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.14%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.T3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance on decryption of historical Borg cipher with multilingual models. Lower is better.</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="S3.T3.8" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T3.8.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.8.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T3.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Error rate (<math id="S3.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.8.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.8.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Seq2Seq <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="S3.T3.8.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">5.47%</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.8.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.8.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Causal LM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite></th>
<td id="S3.T3.8.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4.10%</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.8.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.8.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S3.T3.8.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Base</span> (this work)</th>
<td id="S3.T3.8.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2.80%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The difficulty of decrypting a cryptogram depends strongly on ciphertext length. Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Bijective decoding ‣ 3.1 Ablations ‣ 3 Numerical experiments ‣ Alice: An Interpretable Neural Architecture for Generalization in Substitution Ciphers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows model error distributions across binned lengths.
For very short sequences (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{&lt;}30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{&lt;}30</annotation></semantics></math> characters), the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Base</span> often makes <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5+</annotation></semantics></math> errors—consistent with the unicity distance of English under substitution ciphers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, which implies such ciphertexts cannot be uniquely decrypted.
Beyond this threshold, performance improves rapidly: by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}75</annotation></semantics></math> characters, over 90% of sequences contain at most one error, plateauing at <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}150</annotation></semantics></math> characters where the model is error-free <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}95\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the time.
Residual errors at long lengths appear due to dataset noise (e.g., typos, improper word breaks).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.2 Model performance ‣ 3 Numerical experiments ‣ Alice: An Interpretable Neural Architecture for Generalization in Substitution Ciphers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compares <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice</span> to prior neural deciphering methods.
On short sequences (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{&lt;}128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{&lt;}128</annotation></semantics></math> chars), <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Base</span> achieves a new state of the art, reducing error rates by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="84.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>84.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">84.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> relative to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aldarrab and May [2]</a></cite>.
Notably, it outperforms specialized frequency- and recurrence-based models despite using no handcrafted encoding, and even <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Bijective</span>—less accurate but more interpretable—beats all prior baselines.
With error rates of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{-}2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{-}2\%</annotation></semantics></math>, a cipher of length 50 typically contains just a single mistake, still easily interpretable by humans.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice</span> is not only more accurate but also orders of magnitude faster than the previously fastest algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> (see Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Decoding speed ‣ Alice: An Interpretable Neural Architecture for Generalization in Substitution Ciphers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Multilingual decryption</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Base</span> on the multilingual dataset described above.
During both training and inference time, the information about the language of the text is not provided to the model.
As such, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice</span> now needs to perform the additional task of identifying the correct underlying plaintext language in order to properly decode the ciphertext.
To compare with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kambhatla et al. [12]</a></cite>, we evaluate this model on a length-256 cipher from page 0011v of the historical Borg cipher,<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20240920225756/https://www.su.se/polopoly_fs/1.689014.1699461276!/menu/standard/file/corrected-Latin-translation.txt" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://web.archive.org/web/20240920225756/https://www.su.se/polopoly_fs/1.689014.1699461276!/menu/standard/file/corrected-Latin-translation.txt</a></span></span></span> a 17th century book handwritten in encrypted Latin text, first automatically decrypted by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aldarrab [1]</a></cite>.
We report the performance of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice</span> as compared to previous work in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.2 Model performance ‣ 3 Numerical experiments ‣ Alice: An Interpretable Neural Architecture for Generalization in Substitution Ciphers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with two caveats.
Firstly, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kambhatla et al. [12]</a></cite> and compare against the performance of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aldarrab and May [2]</a></cite>, but we note that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aldarrab and May [2]</a></cite> decrypt a slightly different input, corresponding to page 0002r of the Borg cipher.
Secondly, the error rate of our model varies slightly depending on the transcription of the cipher.
That is, a different ciphertext tokenization mapping results in slightly different outputs from the model.
We thus report the mean of 100 runs, although performance on individual runs varies from 0.00% to 6.25%.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Model generalization</h2>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07282v1/loss_acc.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/189;" width="452" height="189"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Generalization experiments. <span id="S4.F3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Training loss vs. step with a Savitzky-Golay filter applied for smoothing. <span id="S4.F3.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Validation accuracy vs. training step. <span id="S4.F3.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Base</span> trained on fewer ciphers achieves lower training error but does not generalize to unseen ciphers. The number of training ciphers needed to generalize to near perfect accuracy on unseen ciphers is very low (<math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>1500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1500</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate how the number of distinct ciphers (“tasks”) seen during training affects generalization ciphers.
We note here that this task space contains 26! possible ciphers, making memorization infeasible; thus, strong performance on unseen ciphers indicates true generalization.
If the model has learned a general “cryptogram-solving solution,” then accuracy on held-out ciphers should remain high.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We run experiments in training <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Base</span> by varying the number of training ciphers, which are drawn from a limited pool: 10, 100, 250, 500, 750, 1000, 1500, 2500, 5000, and 10000.
For each training example, a cipher mapping is sampled from the pool.
The total number of training examples and steps is fixed; only the number of unique ciphers changes.
Validation uses random ciphers generated from the set of all possible ciphers.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          Given that there are 26! possible ciphers, the probability of using a cipher during validation that was already seen during training is essentially zero (<math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}2.5\times 10^{-27}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>2.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}2.5\times 10^{-27}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></span></span>
All experiments use our 85M parameter <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Base</span> model trained for 50K training steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Model generalization ‣ Alice: An Interpretable Neural Architecture for Generalization in Substitution Ciphers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, generalization emerges between 1000 and 1500 ciphers: the 1500-cipher model achieves high validation accuracy, while the 1000-cipher model does not.
Remarkably, this success occurs despite training on only <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1500/26!=3.7\times 10^{-24}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1500</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mn>26</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3.7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1500/26!=3.7\times 10^{-24}</annotation></semantics></math> of all possible ciphers.
Interestingly, all models achieve low training loss, but those that fail to generalize do so much faster, suggesting reliance on memorization rather than a general solution.
Additionally, models trained on intermediate numbers of ciphers (750 and 1000) show initially high validation accuracy that quickly decays before recovering partially, but never fully.
This dynamic resembles findings in recent in-context learning work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, where models prefer a simple generalization strategy at first, but later drift towards memorization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F4.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="42.43" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 504.25 42.43" width="504.25"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,42.43) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(252.13,0) translate(0,21.22) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -247.51 -3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:48.68em;--ltx-fo-height:1.97em;--ltx-fo-depth:1.29em;font-size:7.35pt;" height="33.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 20.06)" width="495.03"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<table id="S4.F4.pic1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.F4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.F4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S4.F4.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Plaintext:</span></td>
<td id="S4.F4.pic1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S4.F4.pic1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">IN LIFE, WE MAKE THE BEST DECISIONS WE CAN WITH THE INFORMATION WE HAVE ON HAND.</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.F4.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.F4.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S4.F4.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Ciphertext:</span></td>
<td id="S4.F4.pic1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S4.F4.pic1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">RJ HRIF, YF QDAF SEF BFVS KFTRVRNJV YF TDJ YRSE SEF RJINLQDSRNJ YF EDZF NJ EDJK.</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table><span id="S4.F4.pic1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></svg></span>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2509.07282v1/recovered_mapping.png" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/498;" width="476" height="498" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Directly modelling and extraction of latent permutation (mapping).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2509.07282v1/attn_map_example.png" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/507;" width="476" height="507" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Row-normalized attention maps from different model layers and attention heads.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of key recovery via our bijective decoding head (<a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4 Model generalization ‣ Alice: An Interpretable Neural Architecture for Generalization in Substitution Ciphers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>), which is straightforward, and via traditional attention map analysis (<a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4 Model generalization ‣ Alice: An Interpretable Neural Architecture for Generalization in Substitution Ciphers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>), which can be difficult and unreliable.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Interpretability</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cipher mapping recovery</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Of interest when solving cryptograms is the recovery of the cipher key/mapping.
With <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Alice-Bijective</span>, because we explicitly model the latent permutation of the alphabet, we directly recover the key.
In contrast, a Transformer-based model without the bijective decoding head must resort to strategies such as analysis of attention maps (as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a></cite>).
However, attention maps have been shown to be an unreliable indicator of input importance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
While we show the attention maps for only a subset of layers and attention heads, they are already difficult to analyze; the maps must be analyzed individually, as averaging them led to information loss due to mixing between layers.
With the full 12 layers and 12 heads of the model, there are 144 maps to analyze per example, making reliable key extraction with attention maps infeasable.</p>
</div>
